issue.body:
Hello,

This issue was written with the help of AI. I was investigating why supernova took so long to boot on my Mac with many UGen extensions installed. With a few changes, supernova now boots almost as fast as scsynth here. I have split the improvements into separate issues, as small as possible. This is the third one (see also #7731 and #7732). I am not sure each fix is the best way to do it, but together they make booting much faster and remove unnecessary notifications.

## Environment

* SuperCollider version: Latest Dev
* Operating system: macOS 27, Apple M1 Max
* Other details (Qt version, audio driver, etc.): supernova server

## Summary

Supernova opens its UDP port before loading plugins and starting audio, but it only processes incoming messages once audio runs. Every message received during boot is queued and executed in one burst when the server becomes ready. sclang polls `/status` during boot, so it receives a burst of stale replies and reacts to each one. The proposed fix ignores messages received before the server is ready, as scsynth does.

## Steps to reproduce

Run this in the IDE. It starts supernova with the core plugins only, sends `/status` every 50 ms until the first reply, then counts the replies:

```supercollider
(
// Polls /status every 50 ms while supernova boots (core plugins only),
// then counts how many replies arrive at once when it becomes ready.
var addr = NetAddr("127.0.0.1", 57999);
var replies = List[], sent = 0, pid, poll, osc;
osc = OSCFunc({ replies.add(Main.elapsedTime) }, '/status.reply', addr);
pid = ("exec" + (Platform.resourceDir +/+ "supernova").shellQuote
    + "-u 57999 -U" + (Platform.resourceDir +/+ "plugins").shellQuote).unixCmd;
poll = Routine {
    loop { if(replies.isEmpty) { addr.sendMsg('/status'); sent = sent + 1 }; 0.05.wait }
}.play(SystemClock);
SystemClock.sched(8, {
    poll.stop; osc.free; ("kill" + pid).unixCmd;
    "/status sent before the first reply: %".format(sent).postln;
    "replies received in total: %".format(replies.size).postln;
    "replies received in the first 100 ms: %"
        .format(replies.count { |t| t - replies.first < 0.1 }).postln;
});
)
```

## Expected vs. actual behavior

**Expected:** like scsynth, requests sent before the server is ready get no reply. The first request after that gets one reply.

**Actual (develop):** every request that reached the open port is answered, all at the same moment:

```
/status sent before the first reply: 93
replies received in total: 67
replies received in the first 100 ms: 67
```

(The other 26 requests were sent before the port was open and were lost.)

**With the fix:**

```
/status sent before the first reply: 82
replies received in total: 1
replies received in the first 100 ms: 1
```

In sclang, each stale `/status.reply` triggers a new `/notify` while the client is not registered yet. On a boot that takes a few seconds, the post window shows several `Requested notification messages from server 'localhost'` lines, `ServerBoot` runs several times, and fixed node ids collide (`node id N already in use`).

## Problem

**Files:** `server/supernova/server/server.cpp`, `server/supernova/server/main.cpp`, `server/supernova/sc/sc_osc_handler.cpp`

1. The `nova_server` constructor opens the sockets (through the `sc_osc_handler` constructor) and starts the network thread (`server.cpp`, line 80):

```cpp
    if (!args.non_rt)
        start_receive_thread();
```

2. Only after that, `supernova_main()` loads plugins and synthdefs and starts audio (`main.cpp`, lines 364 to 375):

```cpp
        nova_server server(args);             // port open, messages received
        ...
        set_plugin_paths(args, sc_factory.get());
        load_synthdefs(server, args);
        ...
                start_audio_backend(args);
                // Server is ready!
                bootDelayWarningTimer.stop();
                cout << "Supernova ready" << endl;
```

3. Every received message is handed to the audio thread (`sc_osc_handler.cpp`, `handle_packet_async()`, line 834), which only runs once audio has started:

```cpp
    instance->add_sync_callback(p);
```

So all messages received during plugin loading wait in a queue and are executed together when audio starts. scsynth does not do this: it only opens its port once it is fully initialized.

## Possible Fix

Keep opening the port early, so that a port already in use still fails immediately with `address in use`, but ignore messages until the server is ready. 3 files, line numbers from develop 5d47207bb.

**1. `server/supernova/sc/sc_osc_handler.hpp`, line 21:** include `<atomic>`.

```diff
@@ -19,5 +19,6 @@
 #pragma once

 #include <algorithm>
+#include <atomic>
 #include <mutex>
 #include <vector>
```

**2. `server/supernova/sc/sc_osc_handler.hpp`, after line 194 and after line 354:** add a flag and the method that sets it.

```diff
@@ -192,6 +193,11 @@
     }

     void start_receive_thread(void) { detail::network_thread::start_receive(); }

+    /* Packets received before this are dropped: the socket is bound early to detect a busy port,
+     * but nothing is processed until plugins are loaded and the audio backend runs (as with scsynth,
+     * whose port only opens at that point). */
+    void start_accepting_packets(void) { accepting_packets_.store(true, std::memory_order_release); }
+
     typedef osc::ReceivedPacket ReceivedPacket;
     typedef osc::ReceivedBundle ReceivedBundle;
```

```diff
@@ -352,5 +358,6 @@

     tcp::acceptor tcp_acceptor_;
     const char* tcp_password_; /* we are not owning this! */
+    std::atomic<bool> accepting_packets_ { false };

     std::array<char, 1 << 15> recv_buffer_;
```

**3. `server/supernova/sc/sc_osc_handler.cpp`, line 817:** ignore messages while the flag is not set.

```diff
@@ -816,3 +816,6 @@
 void sc_osc_handler::handle_packet_async(const char* data, size_t length, endpoint_ptr const& endpoint) {
+    if (!accepting_packets_.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
+        return;
+
     received_packet* p = received_packet::alloc_packet(data, length, endpoint);
     if (!p)
```

**4. `server/supernova/server/main.cpp`, line 373:** set the flag once audio has started, just before `Supernova ready`.

```diff
@@ -371,5 +371,6 @@
             try {
                 start_audio_backend(args);
                 // Server is ready!
+                server.start_accepting_packets();
                 bootDelayWarningTimer.stop();
                 cout << "Supernova ready" << endl;
```

## What the fix changes

* **No burst of stale replies.** Messages sent during boot are ignored, as with scsynth. Only requests sent after `Supernova ready` get a reply.
* **One `/notify` per boot in sclang.** sclang no longer receives dozens of `/status.reply` at once, so it normally sends a single `/notify` and runs `ServerBoot` once. The `node id N already in use` errors caused by this burst disappear.
* **Faster boot from sclang.** Because stale `/status` messages are dropped (change 3), sclang gets one reply at a time, normally sends a single `/notify`, and runs `prFinalizeBoot` (`ServerBoot.run` and `initTree`, `ServerStatus.sc` lines 242-244) once instead of once per stale reply.
* **Port conflicts still detected immediately.** The port is still opened at the start, so a second supernova on the same port still fails at once with `address in use`, before loading any plugin.
* **No change after boot, and none in non-realtime mode.** Once ready, every message is processed as before. Non-realtime mode does not use `handle_packet_async()`.

Related: #7731, #7732 (the causes of slow supernova boot on macOS, which make this burst larger).

Hello,

This issue was written with the help of AI. I was investigating why supernova took so long to boot on my Mac with many UGen extensions installed. With a few changes, supernova now boots almost as fast as scsynth here. I have split the improvements into separate issues, as small as possible. This is the fourth one (see also #7731, #7732 and #7733). I am not sure each fix is the best way to do it, but together they make booting much faster and remove unnecessary notifications.

## Environment

* SuperCollider version: Latest Dev
* Operating system: macOS 27, Apple M1 Max
* Other details (Qt version, audio driver, etc.): supernova server

## Summary

While the server boots, sclang sends a new `/notify` for every `/status.reply` it receives until it is registered. Each attempt adds one more `/done` listener without removing the previous ones. When `/done` arrives, all of these listeners fire, and each one runs `prFinalizeBoot`, so `ServerBoot` and `initTree` run many times. The proposed fix sends one `/notify` at a time and keeps it pending until boot is finalized.

This is the client side of #7733: #7733 stops supernova from sending a burst of stale replies, and this fix makes sclang robust to any server that answers several `/status` before `/notify`. Each fix alone removes the problem.

## Steps to reproduce

Run this in the IDE, with the supernova from develop. It boots supernova with the core plugins only and counts how many times `ServerBoot` runs:

```supercollider
(
// Boots supernova (core plugins only) and counts how many times ServerBoot runs.
var bootRuns = 0, action = { bootRuns = bootRuns + 1 };
Server.supernova;
s.options.ugenPluginsPath = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "plugins";
s.aliveThreadPeriod = 0.05; // poll /status more often, as some setups do
ServerBoot.add(action, s);
s.waitForBoot({
    1.wait;
    "ServerBoot ran % times".format(bootRuns).postln;
    ServerBoot.remove(action, s);
});
)
```

## Expected vs. actual behavior

**Expected:** one `/notify`, and `ServerBoot` runs once.

**Actual (develop):**

```
Requested notification messages from server 'localhost'
Requested notification messages from server 'localhost'
...                                   (78 lines)
node id 1 already in use
node id 2 already in use
...                                   (76 times each)
ServerBoot ran 77 times
```

**With the fix:**

```
Requested notification messages from server 'localhost'
ServerBoot ran 1 times
```

## Problem

**File:** `SCClassLibrary/Common/Control/ServerStatus.sc`

1. Line 127, in `addStatusWatcher`: every `/status.reply` sends a `/notify` while `notified` is false.

```supercollider
if(notify and: { notified.not }) { this.prSendNotifyRequest(true,true) };
```

2. Line 292, in `prSendNotifyRequest`: every call registers a new `/done` listener, and the previous ones stay registered.

3. Line 308: when `/done` arrives, every registered listener matches it and calls `prFinalizeBoot`, which runs `ServerBoot.run` and `initTree` (lines 242-244). Each extra `initTree` recreates groups 1 and 2, hence `node id 1 already in use` and `node id 2 already in use`.

`notified` only becomes true at the end of `prFinalizeBoot`, after `ServerBoot`, `sync` and `initTree`. Until then, every `/status.reply` sends yet another `/notify`.

## Possible Fix

**File to modify:** `SCClassLibrary/Common/Control/ServerStatus.sc`, 5 places (line numbers from develop 5d47207bb).

**1. After line 13:** a flag for the pending request, and the listeners of the last request.

```diff
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@ ServerStatusWatcher {
 	var <avgCPU, <peakCPU;
 	var <sampleRate, <actualSampleRate;

+	var pendingNotifyRequest = false, prevDoneOSCFunc, prevFailOSCFunc;
 	var reallyDeadCount = 0, bootNotifyFirst = true;
 	var <>timeoutBeforeConsideredDeadOnExit = 3.0;

```

**2. After line 34, in `quit`:** reset them.

```diff
@@ -32,6 +33,9 @@ ServerStatusWatcher {
 		notified = false;
 		serverBooting = false;
 		unresponsive = false;
+		pendingNotifyRequest = false;
+		prevDoneOSCFunc.free;
+		prevFailOSCFunc.free;

 		// server.changed(\serverRunning) should be deferred in dependants!
 		//  just in case some don't, defer here to avoid gui updates breaking.
```

**3. After line 246, in `prFinalizeBoot`:** the request stops being pending only once the client is fully registered.

```diff
@@ -244,6 +248,7 @@ ServerStatusWatcher {
 			server.initTree;

 			this.notified = true;
+			pendingNotifyRequest = false;
 			server.changed(\serverRunning);
 		}, AppClock)
 	}
```

**4. After line 289, in `prSendNotifyRequest`:** send nothing while a request is pending, and free the previous listeners.

```diff
@@ -288,6 +293,16 @@ ServerStatusWatcher {

 		if(hasBooted.not) { ^this };

+		// A request is already in flight (or boot is being finalized): don't send another one
+		// for each /status.reply, or every stacked /done listener would run prFinalizeBoot.
+		if(flag and: { pendingNotifyRequest }) { ^this };
+
+		// clean up any previous unresolved listeners before registering new ones
+		prevDoneOSCFunc.free;
+		prevFailOSCFunc.free;
+
+		if(flag) { pendingNotifyRequest = true };
+
 		// set up oscfuncs for possible server responses, \done or \failed
 		doneOSCFunc = OSCFunc({ |msg|
 			var newClientID = msg[2], newMaxLogins = msg[3];
```

**5. Lines 307 to 326, in the `/done` and `/fail` listeners:** clear the flag in every case that does not go through `prFinalizeBoot`, and remember the new listeners.

```diff
@@ -305,24 +320,30 @@ ServerStatusWatcher {
 				// XXX: this is a workaround because using `serverBooting` is not reliable
 				// when server is rebooted quickly.
 				if(addingStatusWatcher) {
-					this.prFinalizeBoot;
+					this.prFinalizeBoot; // clears pendingNotifyRequest once notified
 				} {
 					notified = true;
+					pendingNotifyRequest = false;
 				}

 			} {
 				notified = false;
+				pendingNotifyRequest = false;
 			};

 		}, '/done', server.addr, argTemplate:['/notify', nil]).oneShot;

 		failOSCFunc = OSCFunc({|msg|

+			pendingNotifyRequest = false;
 			doneOSCFunc.free;
 			server.prHandleNotifyFailString(msg[2], msg);

 		}, '/fail', server.addr, argTemplate:['/notify', nil, nil]).oneShot;

+		prevDoneOSCFunc = doneOSCFunc;
+		prevFailOSCFunc = failOSCFunc;
+
 		server.sendMsg("/notify", flag.binaryValue, server.clientID);

 		if(flag){
```

## What the fix changes

* **One `/notify` per boot.** sclang waits for the answer to its request instead of sending one per `/status.reply`.
* **`ServerBoot` and `initTree` run once.** Only one `/done` listener exists at a time, so `prFinalizeBoot` runs once.
* **No `node id N already in use` at boot** from repeated `initTree` or `ServerBoot` actions.
* **Faster boot from sclang,** since `ServerBoot` and `initTree` are no longer repeated.
* **Works with any slow server,** not only supernova: a remote server, a busy machine or a short `aliveThreadPeriod` no longer cause repeated registrations.
* **No change otherwise.** Turning notifications off and on, quitting and rebooting in the same session behave as before (tested).

Related: #7733 (server side of the same problem), #7731, #7732.

Hello,

This issue was written with the help of AI. I was investigating why supernova took so long to boot on my Mac with many UGen extensions installed. With a few changes, supernova now boots almost as fast as scsynth here. I have split the improvements into separate issues, as small as possible. This is the fifth one (see also #7731, #7732, #7733 and #7734). This one is not about speed: it fixes a reboot that fails without any message, with scsynth as well as supernova.

## Environment

* SuperCollider version: Latest Dev
* Operating system: macOS 27, Apple M1 Max
* Other details (Qt version, audio driver, etc.): supernova and scsynth, many third-party plugins installed

## Summary

When `Server:boot` finds that the previous server process has not exited yet, it waits for it, but only for 1 second. If the process takes longer, `boot` gives up without posting anything: no `Booting server` line, no error, and the server stays off.

With many plugins installed, both scsynth and supernova can take a few seconds to exit after `/quit`. `s.reboot` then fails silently every time. The proposed fix waits up to 15 seconds, then warns and boots anyway.

## Steps to reproduce

Run this in the IDE with the server stopped. It makes sclang believe that a previous server process is still running, by giving it the pid of a process that does not exit, then boots:

```supercollider
(
// Simulates a previous server process that has not exited yet, then boots.
Server.supernova;
s.instVarPut(\pid, "sleep 60".unixCmd);
s.doWhenBooted({ "booted".postln });
s.boot;
SystemClock.sched(40, { "after 40 s: serverRunning = %".format(s.serverRunning).postln });
)
```

In real use, the same happens with `s.reboot`, or `s.quit; s.boot;`, whenever the server takes more than 1 second to exit.

## Expected vs. actual behavior

**Expected:** the server boots, or at least a message explains why it does not.

**Actual (develop):** nothing at all is posted, not even `Booting server`:

```
after 40 s: serverRunning = false
```

**With the fix:**

```
WARNING: server 'localhost': previous process took longer than 15 s to fully exit; booting anyway.
Booting server 'localhost' on address 127.0.0.1:57110.
...
booted
after 40 s: serverRunning = true
```

Real case, with many plugins installed:

| | develop | with the fix |
|---|---|---|
| supernova, `s.reboot` or `s.quit; s.boot;` | nothing posted, server stays off | reboots normally |
| scsynth, `s.reboot` or `s.quit; s.boot;` | nothing posted, server stays off | reboots normally |

In this real case the warning is not even needed: with the longer wait, sclang sees the old process exit after a few seconds and boots normally.

## Problem

**File:** `SCClassLibrary/Common/Control/Server.sc`

In `boot` (line 856), lines 882 to 886 call `prWaitForPidRelease` with a single block:

```supercollider
this.prWaitForPidRelease {
    this.bootServerApp({
        if(startAliveThread) { statusWatcher.startAliveThread }
    })
};
```

`prWaitForPidRelease { |onComplete, onFailure, timeout = 1|` (line 891) receives this block as `onComplete`. `onFailure` stays `nil` and `timeout` stays 1 second. If `pid` is still set after 1 second, `prWaitForPidRelease` calls `onFailure.value` (line 913). `nil.value` does nothing, so `bootServerApp` is never called and nothing is posted.

The method already has a `FIXME` about the supernova reboot hang on macOS (line 898), which is why this wait exists. The other caller, `quitSync` (line 1063), passes an `onFailure` and a longer timeout. `boot` is the only caller without them.

## Possible Fix

**File to modify:** `SCClassLibrary/Common/Control/Server.sc`, lines 882 to 886 (line numbers from develop 5d47207bb).

Pass an `onFailure` that warns and boots anyway, and a 15 s timeout:

```diff
@@ -879,11 +879,19 @@ Server {
 				this.quit;
 				this.boot;
 			}, {
-				this.prWaitForPidRelease {
+				this.prWaitForPidRelease({
 					this.bootServerApp({
 						if(startAliveThread) { statusWatcher.startAliveThread }
 					})
-				};
+				}, {
+					// prPingApp found nothing answering at this address: the previous
+					// process is only slow to exit. Boot anyway instead of silently giving up.
+					"server '%': previous process took longer than % s to fully "
+					"exit; booting anyway.".format(this.name, 15).warn;
+					this.bootServerApp({
+						if(startAliveThread) { statusWatcher.startAliveThread }
+					})
+				}, 15);
 			}, 0.25);
 		}
 	}
```

Booting anyway is reasonably safe: `prPingApp`, just before, has already found nothing answering at this address. If the port is really still in use, the server then fails with a visible `address in use` error instead of silence.

**Why 15 s:** it is a maximum, not a fixed delay. `prWaitForPidRelease` returns as soon as the old process has exited, so a normal reboot waits only as long as the exit takes. 15 s leaves a comfortable margin over the exit times observed, including the slowest one (a supernova without #7731, which took more than 10 seconds to exit). A much longer value would only delay the warning when the old process is really stuck.

## What the fix changes

* **No more silent failure.** `boot` either boots, or posts a warning followed by a normal boot attempt.
* **`s.reboot` works with slow-exiting servers.** Waiting up to 15 s instead of 1 s covers the exit time of both servers with many plugins.
* **No delay in the normal case.** When the old process exits quickly, the boot starts as soon as it is gone, as before.
* **Fixes scsynth too.** The fix is in sclang, and scsynth with many plugins also takes more than 1 second to exit.

Related: #7731 (the priority fix also shortens supernova's exit time a lot), #7732, #7733, #7734.

Hello,

This issue was written with the help of AI. I was investigating why supernova took so long to boot on my Mac with many UGen extensions installed. With a few changes, supernova now boots almost as fast as scsynth here. I have split the improvements into separate issues, as small as possible (see also #7731, #7732, #7733, #7734 and #7735). This one is not about speed: it removes unnecessary messages that scsynth does not print. I do not know if it is the best way to solve these unnecessary messages or if if has other performance consequences. I will stop here concerning Supernova.

## Environment

* SuperCollider version: Latest Dev
* Operating system: macOS 27, Apple M1 Max
* Other details (Qt version, audio driver, etc.): supernova server, compared with scsynth

## Summary

Code sometimes sends `/g_new` for a group that already exists, on purpose, to make sure the group exists. When the group is already at the requested place, the result is exactly what was asked for, so scsynth accepts it silently. supernova prints `node id N already in use` instead.

This is not a real error: nothing is missing and nothing breaks. But code that does this on every node tree initialization produces dozens of these lines in a supernova session, which can hide real errors in the post window. The proposed fix gives supernova the same rule as scsynth.

## Steps to reproduce

Run this in the IDE:

```supercollider
(
// Sends /g_new for groups that already exist at the same place.
Server.supernova;
s.waitForBoot({
    s.sendMsg(\g_new, 99999, 1, 1); s.sync;  // create group 99999 at the tail of group 1
    s.sendMsg(\g_new, 99999, 1, 1); s.sync;  // same group, same place, again
    s.sendMsg(\g_new, 99998, 3, 1); s.sync;  // create group 99998 after group 1
    s.sendMsg(\g_new, 99998, 3, 1); s.sync;  // same group, same place, again
    1.wait; "--- done".postln;
});
)
```

## Expected vs. actual behavior

**Expected:** no message, as with scsynth. Both groups already exist at the requested place.

**Actual (develop):**

```
node id 99999 already in use
node id 99998 already in use
```

**With the fix:** no message, same as scsynth.

A real conflict is still reported. Sending `/g_new` with an existing id at another place, for example `s.sendMsg(\g_new, 99999, 1, 0)` after the code above, still prints `node id 99999 already in use`. scsynth still reports it too, with `FAILURE IN SERVER /g_new duplicate node ID`.

| | supernova develop | supernova with the fix | scsynth |
|---|---|---|---|
| existing group, same place (head, tail, before, after) | message | no message | no message |
| existing id, different place | message | message | message |

## Problem

**File:** `server/supernova/sc/sc_osc_handler.cpp`, `handle_g_new()` (line 1269)

Every existing id is rejected by `check_node_id()` (line 1278), which prints the message (line 88):

```cpp
if (node_id == -1)
    node_id = instance->generate_node_id();
else if (!check_node_id(node_id))
    continue;
```

scsynth, in `meth_g_new()` (`server/scsynth/SC_MiscCmds.cpp`, lines 849, 865, 881 and 898), accepts an existing id when it is a group that already has the expected parent, for the four add actions head, tail, before and after:

```cpp
if (err == kSCErr_DuplicateNodeID) {
    newGroup = World_GetGroup(inWorld, newGroupID);
    if (!newGroup || !newGroup->mNode.mParent || newGroup->mNode.mParent != group)
        return err;
}
```

## Possible Fix

**File to modify:** `server/supernova/sc/sc_osc_handler.cpp`, `handle_g_new()`, after line 1274 (line numbers from develop 5d47207bb).

Before rejecting an existing id, accept it silently if it is a group that already has the expected parent, like scsynth:

```diff
@@ -1273,6 +1273,21 @@
         osc::int32 node_id, action, target_id;
         args >> node_id >> action >> target_id;

+        // Like scsynth: /g_new on a group that already exists under the same parent is a no-op,
+        // so clients can send it to "make sure the group exists".
+        if (node_id != -1 && !instance->node_id_available(node_id)) {
+            abstract_group* existing = instance->find_group(node_id);
+            abstract_group* parent = nullptr;
+            if (action == head || action == tail)
+                parent = instance->find_group(target_id);
+            else if (action == before || action == after) {
+                server_node* target = instance->find_node(target_id);
+                parent = target ? target->get_parent() : nullptr;
+            }
+            if (existing && parent && existing->get_parent() == parent)
+                continue;
+        }
+
         if (node_id == -1)
             node_id = instance->generate_node_id();
         else if (!check_node_id(node_id))
```

As in scsynth, the existing group is left where it is: it is not moved, and nothing is created.

## What the fix changes

* **Same behavior as scsynth** for `/g_new` on an existing group, for the four add actions.
* **No more unnecessary messages** when code makes sure a group exists. The post window stays readable, and real errors are no longer hidden among them.
* **Real conflicts are still reported:** an existing id at another place, or an existing synth with the same id.
* **No change in sound or speed.** Only the messages change: the groups already existed.

Related: #7731, #7732, #7733, #7734, #7735.

Hello,

This issue was written with the help of AI. It is the scsynth counterpart of #7732: the same wasted work happens in scsynth's plugin loader, in the other direction. Fixing it saves up to a few seconds at every scsynth boot, especially with many extensions installed. I do not know if it is the best way to solve it, but booting is really faster now at least to me, with lots of extensions.

## Environment

* SuperCollider version: Latest Dev
* Operating system: macOS 27, Apple M1 Max
* Other details (Qt version, audio driver, etc.): scsynth server

## Summary

Plugins ship as pairs, one build per server, in the same folder (`BinaryOpUGens.scx` and `BinaryOpUGens_supernova.scx`). At boot, scsynth fully opens the supernova build of every pair with `dlopen`, only to close it again. The proposed fix skips a `_supernova` build without opening it when its scsynth version is in the same folder.

## Steps to reproduce

Run this in the IDE. It starts scsynth with the core plugins only, for 3 seconds, and lists the supernova builds it opens:

```supercollider
(
// Lists the supernova builds that scsynth opens at boot (core plugins only).
var dir = Platform.resourceDir.shellQuote;
